  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a simple workflow for turning nanoscale particles into targeted or delivery-ready biological tools. At the top, the starting materials are basic nanoparticles, shown as either a spherical quantum dot or an elongated nanorod. These particles are then coated with protective linker molecules, such as thiol-linked PEG or peptides, which appear to form a stabilizing layer around the particle surface.\n\nFrom this protected intermediate, the particles can be further functionalized with different types of biological or chemical cargo. The lower part of the diagram shows several examples: antibodies, biotin, peptides, small drugs or other molecules, and DNA/RNA. The arrows suggest that the same nanoparticle platform can be adapted into multiple end uses depending on what is attached to the surface.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys a modular surface-engineering strategy: begin with a nanoparticle core, add protective linkers to improve compatibility and provide attachment points, and then decorate the particle with specific molecules for targeting, sensing, or delivery. The main message is the versatility of nanomaterials as customizable carriers for biomedical applications.",
